    hpi: "L.K., 67-year-old Bank Teller from Voi, presents with retrosternal burning sensation (heartburn) occurring 30-60 minutes after meals, worse on lying down or bending forward. Reports acid regurgitation leaving a sour taste, occasional difficulty swallowing (dysphagia) with solid food, and chronic cough (worse at night). Symptoms started 2 months ago and have been increasing in frequency (now daily). Uses over-the-counter antacids with minimal relief. BMI 31 kg/m².",
    pmh: "Mild intermittent asthma. No prior GI surgery.. Also has Type 2 Diabetes.",
    medHx: "No regular medications",
    allergies: "NKDA",
    pe: "Overweight (BMI 31). Oral cavity: dental enamel erosion noted. Mild halitosis. Abdomen: soft, non-tender. No epigastric masses. No hepatosplenomegaly.",
    vitals: "HR: 93 bpm, BP: 99/76 mmHg, RR: 20 breaths/min, Temp: 38.2°C, SpO2: 90%",
    labs: "Hb 13.5 g/dL. H. pylori stool antigen: Negative. OGD (if performed): Los Angeles Grade B oesophagitis (linear erosions <5mm not extending between mucosal folds at distal oesophagus). Biopsy: negative for Barretts or dysplasia.",
    imaging: "No routine imaging. Barium swallow (if dysphagia): normal oesophageal motility, hiatal hernia noted.",
    diagnosis: "Gastro-Oesophageal Reflux Disease with Grade B Reflux Oesophagitis",
    ddx: ["GERD", "Non-Erosive Reflux Disease", "Functional Dyspepsia", "Oesophageal Spasm", "Peptic Ulcer Disease"],
    goals: "Control symptoms, heal oesophagitis, prevent complications (stricture, Barretts oesophagus, aspiration), improve quality of life",
    pharm: "1. PPI therapy: Omeprazole 20mg daily before breakfast for 8 weeks (or Esomeprazole 40mg daily, Pantoprazole 40mg daily). 2. If partial response: increase to BID dosing. 3. Antacids/alginates PRN for breakthrough symptoms (e.g., Gaviscon Advance 10mL after meals and at bedtime). 4. If nocturnal symptoms despite PPI: add H2RA (Ranitidine 150mg or Famotidine 20mg at bedtime, though Ranitidine is off-market in some regions). 5. Prokinetic agents (Metoclopramide 10mg TID) may be considered but use cautiously due to side effects.",
    nonPharm: "Weight loss (target BMI <25). Elevate head of bed (6-8 inch blocks under headposts). Avoid meals within 3 hours of bedtime. Avoid triggers: fatty foods, chocolate, caffeine, alcohol, carbonated drinks, spicy foods. Stop smoking.",
    carePlan: "Start PPI at standard dose. Lifestyle modifications as above. Review in 4-8 weeks. If symptoms controlled, step down to lowest effective PPI dose (e.g., maintenance Omeprazole 10-20mg daily). If poor response: OGD to assess for complications, pH monitoring, manometry.",
    dtps: "1. Acid reflux causing erosive oesophagitis requiring gastric acid suppression. 2. Modifiable risk factors: obesity and dietary habits.",
    monitoring: "Symptom diary (frequency and severity of heartburn, regurgitation). Watch for alarm symptoms: dysphagia, weight loss, GI bleeding, anaemia. Annual FBC to check for iron deficiency.",
    counselling: "Take PPI 30-60 minutes before breakfast for best effect. PPI is not a \"when needed\" drug — daily adherence is key for healing. Weight loss is the single most effective lifestyle change. Elevate head of bed — use blocks, not extra pillows (pillows increase abdominal pressure).",
    followUp: "Follow-up in 8 weeks. If symptoms recur on step-down PPI, continue lowest effective maintenance dose long-term. Repeat OGD in 3-5 years if Barretts surveillance needed.",
    pearls: "PPIs are the most effective medical therapy for GERD. They should be taken 30-60 min before meals (not at bedtime) for maximal effect on proton pumps activated by food intake. Long-term PPI use (>1 year) warrants monitoring for: B12 deficiency, osteoporosis, hypomagnesaemia, C. difficile, and community-acquired pneumonia.",
    references: ["ACG Guidelines: GERD Management", "Lyon Consensus: GERD Definition", "Kenya Essential Medicines List: GI Medicines"],
