    disease: "Helicobacter pylori Infection",
    title: "H. pylori-Positive Gastritis in a Young Adult with Dyspepsia — Variant 2",
    difficulty: "Beginner",
    demographics: "C.K., 54-year-old male, 77 kg, Farmer from Lodwar",
    chiefComplaint: "Chronic epigastric discomfort, bloating, and belching for 2 months",
    hpi: "C.K., 54-year-old Farmer from Lodwar, presents with long-standing epigastric discomfort described as gnawing, associated with bloating, excessive belching, and early satiety. No specific food triggers. No nocturnal pain. No weight loss. Was treated for \"gastritis\" twice in the past year with antacids and ranitidine but symptoms always returned. No NSAID use.",
    pmh: "No significant PMH.. Also has Hypertension.",
    medHx: "No regular medications. Previous courses of Ranitidine and antacids.",
    allergies: "NKDA",
    pe: "Healthy appearance. Epigastric mild tenderness. No masses. No hepatosplenomegaly.",
    vitals: "HR: 100 bpm, BP: 117/78 mmHg, RR: 22 breaths/min, Temp: 38.4°C, SpO2: 93%",
    labs: "H. pylori stool antigen: Positive. Urea breath test: Positive. Hb 13.8 g/dL (normal). LFTs normal. OGD: diffuse antral gastritis, no ulcers, biopsies taken.",
    imaging: "No imaging indicated.",
    diagnosis: "Chronic H. pylori-Associated Antral Gastritis",
    ddx: ["H. pylori Gastritis", "Non-Ulcer Dyspepsia", "Functional Dyspepsia", "Peptic Ulcer Disease", "Gastric Cancer"],
    goals: "Eradicate H. pylori, resolve gastritis, reduce risk of peptic ulcer and gastric cancer, relieve dyspeptic symptoms",
    pharm: "1. First-line therapy (if no prior macrolide exposure): Clarithromycin-based triple therapy for 14 days: Omeprazole 20mg BID + Amoxicillin 1g BID + Clarithromycin 500mg BID. 2. If penicillin-allergic: Bismuth quadruple therapy: Omeprazole 20mg BID + Bismuth subsalicylate 524mg QID + Metronidazole 500mg TID + Tetracycline 500mg QID for 14 days. 3. Alternative in high clarithromycin resistance: Bismuth quadruple therapy as above.",
    nonPharm: "Avoid NSAIDs and alcohol during treatment. No specific dietary restrictions.",
    carePlan: "Prescribe 14-day eradication therapy. Explain importance of completing full course. Confirm eradication with stool antigen or urea breath test at least 4 weeks after completion. If positive: salvage therapy with bismuth quadruple or levofloxacin-based regimen.",
    dtps: "1. Active H. pylori infection causing chronic gastritis requiring eradication. 2. Risk of progression to PUD, MALT lymphoma, or gastric adenocarcinoma if untreated.",
    monitoring: "Monitor for side effects of antibiotics (diarrhoea, metallic taste, nausea). Urea breath test or stool antigen at 4 weeks post-treatment to confirm eradication.",
    counselling: "Complete ALL 14 days of treatment — even one missed dose can lead to resistance. Avoid alcohol during treatment and for 1 week after (metronidazole interaction). The dark stools from bismuth (if on quadruple therapy) are normal. Report severe diarrhoea or rash.",
    followUp: "Follow-up in 6-8 weeks to review eradication test results. If eradication fails, refer to gastroenterology for resistance-guided therapy.",
    pearls: "Clarithromycin resistance rates in Kenya are rising (estimated 20-30%). Bismuth quadruple therapy is recommended as first-line in areas with >15% clarithromycin resistance. H. pylori is a WHO Class I carcinogen for gastric cancer. Eradication reduces gastric cancer risk in patients with precancerous lesions.",
