    monitoring: "Daily: LFTs, INR, bilirubin, mental status. Platelet count. Monitor for signs of progression to fulminant hepatitis (encephalopathy, rising INR, rapid drop in liver size). HBV DNA at diagnosis. Repeat HBsAg at 6 months to check clearance.",
    counselling: "Hepatitis B is transmitted through blood, sexual contact, and from mother to child. Use condoms consistently until you test negative for HBsAg. Your household contacts and sexual partners need testing and vaccination. Do not drink alcohol for at least 6 months. Rest is important — your liver needs energy to heal.",
    followUp: "Gastroenterology/hepatology clinic in 4 weeks. Repeat LFTs, HBsAg, HBeAg, and HBV DNA in 3 and 6 months. If HBsAg remains positive at 6 months, diagnosis changes to chronic HBV and treatment may be indicated.",
    pearls: "In healthy adults, >95% of acute HBV infections clear spontaneously. Risk of chronic infection is inversely related to age: <5% in adults, 30-50% in children <6 years, 90% in neonates. Tenofovir is not routinely indicated for acute HBV but should be considered in severe/fulminant cases. All pregnant women should be screened for HBsAg — infants of HBsAg+ mothers need HBIG + vaccine within 12 hours.",
